## Abstract

The Hodge Complex Standard Model assigns the fourteen modes at the self-paired eigenvalue of the discrete Laplacian on the periodic eight-by-eight torus to one Standard Model generation. Two order-two operations act naturally on this multiplet: the diagonal reflection, identified in HCSM-26 as the unique chiral reflection, and the half-period translation, the diagonal shift of both mode coordinates by half the torus side length. The two operations commute, and each squares to the identity, so they generate a Klein four-group. This paper proves three theorems about the action of this Klein group on the fourteen-mode multiplet. First, the action has orbit decomposition five size-two orbits plus one size-four orbit, totalling fourteen modes. Second, the pointwise stabilizer of each orbit falls into one of three classes, corresponding to the three non-trivial subgroups of the Klein group. Third, the four-cycle orbit has four mass-ordering values that are simultaneously all framework constants: nine, twelve, fourteen, and twenty-one. These values satisfy two additive identities within the orbit, and their sum equals fifty-six, the dimension of the Hodge sector at the self-paired eigenvalue; their mean equals fourteen, the multiplicity. This paper also proves that the orbit-class swap pair, consisting of the dark photon and the top quark, is the unique edge in the distinguished-pair graph that connects two distinct Klein orbits. All theorems are HCSM-native; all numerical claims are verified at machine precision.

