## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 The Klein group in HCSM

The Hodge Complex Standard Model (HCSM) derives the gauge structure, matter content, particle spectrum, and mixing matrices of the Standard Model from a single discrete substrate: the periodic eight-by-eight torus. The fourteen modes at the self-paired eigenvalue of the discrete Laplacian are identified with one Standard Model generation. Their dihedral representation theory determines the gauge algebra; their mass-ordering labels determine the mass spectrum; the zero-state vector from the fourteen-by-nineteen measurement matrix determines the flavor structure.

Two order-two operations act on the fourteen-mode multiplet. The first is the diagonal reflection, which swaps the two mode coordinates and has been identified in HCSM-26 as the unique chiral reflection of the dihedral group. The second is the half-period translation, which shifts both mode coordinates by half the torus side length. The two operations commute, and each squares to the identity, so they generate a Klein four-group.

This paper proves that the action of the Klein group organizes the fourteen-mode multiplet completely: the orbits are classified, the stabilizers of the orbits are classified, and one orbit in particular — the four-cycle orbit — contains four mass-ordering values that are all framework constants.

### 1.2 The one-line summary

The Klein group is the framework's smallest non-trivial symmetry group that acts on the fourteen-mode multiplet in a way that is neither dihedral nor trivial. Its orbit structure separates the fourteen modes into three regions: four two-cycle orbits with a common stabilizer, an observer orbit fixed by the chiral reflection, and a four-cycle orbit with trivial stabilizer. The four-cycle orbit is the framework-constant orbit.

### 1.3 The three theorems of this paper

The paper is organized around three theorems.

**Theorem 1 — The Klein Orbit Theorem.** The Klein four-group generated by the diagonal reflection and the half-period translation acts on the fourteen-mode multiplet with orbit decomposition consisting of five size-two orbits and one size-four orbit.

**Theorem 2 — The Kernel Classification Theorem.** The pointwise stabilizer of each Klein orbit falls into one of three classes, corresponding to the three non-trivial subgroups of the Klein group.

**Theorem 3 — The Four-Orbit Framework-Constant Theorem.** The four-cycle orbit's four mass-ordering values are all framework constants, and their sum equals fifty-six, the dimension of the Hodge sector at the self-paired eigenvalue.

Section 5 proves a companion theorem — the Swap-Pair Bridge Theorem — identifying the unique inter-orbit edge in the distinguished-pair graph.

