## 2. Setup

This section states the HCSM primitives on which the paper depends. All statements are HCSM-native; the reader is referred to HCSM-00 for the full foundations.

### 2.1 The fourteen-mode multiplet

The substrate is the periodic eight-by-eight torus with sixty-four sites, selected by the photon-helicity anchor and the equal-entropy-spacing anchor. The discrete five-point Laplacian has eigenvalues equal to four minus two times the cosine of pi times n-one over four, minus two times the cosine of pi times n-two over four, for n-one and n-two ranging over the integers zero through seven. The self-paired eigenvalue four has multiplicity fourteen.

In the standard mode order used throughout HCSM — matching the zero-state vector of HCSM-22 — the fourteen modes are: neutrino, electron, up quark, down quark, strange quark, muon, dark photon, charm quark, tau, bottom quark, W boson, Z boson, Higgs, top quark.

The mode coordinates in this order are:

- Neutrino: (zero, four).
- Electron: (four, zero).
- Up quark: (one, three).
- Down quark: (one, five).
- Strange quark: (three, one).
- Muon: (three, seven).
- Dark photon: (five, seven).
- Charm quark: (seven, three).
- Tau: (seven, five).
- Bottom quark: (five, one).
- W boson: (two, two).
- Z boson: (two, six).
- Higgs: (six, two).
- Top quark: (six, six).

### 2.2 The diagonal reflection

The diagonal reflection is the map that sends a mode at coordinates (a, b) to the mode at coordinates (b, a), modulo eight. It is an element of the dihedral point group of the square, and its square is the identity. It has been identified in HCSM-26 as the unique chiral reflection of the dihedral group: the unique element that fixes exactly the pair at the two diagonal fixed points on the fourteen-mode multiplet and anticommutes with the winding generator.

The fixed set of the diagonal reflection on the fourteen-mode multiplet is exactly the observer pair consisting of the W boson and the top quark.

### 2.3 The half-period translation

The half-period translation is the map that sends a mode at coordinates (a, b) to the mode at coordinates (a plus four, b plus four), modulo eight. It shifts both mode coordinates by half the torus side length, which is four. Its square is the identity.

### 2.4 The Klein four-group

**Lemma (Klein structure).** The diagonal reflection and the half-period translation commute and each squares to the identity. Hence they generate a Klein four-group consisting of the identity, the diagonal reflection, the half-period translation, and the product of the two.

**Proof.** The relations that the diagonal reflection and the half-period translation each square to the identity are immediate from the definitions. For the commutator: applying the diagonal reflection after the half-period translation to a mode at coordinates (a, b) gives the mode at (b plus four, a plus four). Applying the half-period translation after the diagonal reflection to the same mode gives the same result. The two expressions coincide, so the two operations commute. The group generated by two commuting involutions is the Klein four-group; this is verified at machine precision in the companion verification script.

### 2.5 The product of the two generators

The product of the two generators is the map that sends a mode at coordinates (a, b) to the mode at coordinates (b plus four, a plus four), modulo eight. It is an involution, and it is not equal to either the diagonal reflection or the half-period translation.

### 2.6 The mass-ordering labels

The mass-ordering labels — the N-over-four values — are derived from the mode coordinates by the delta function of HCSM-23. In the standard mode order, the labels are: sixty, twenty-four, twenty-one, twenty, fourteen, fourteen, twelve, nine point five, nine, seven point five, two, two, one, zero point five.

Throughout this paper, we refer to these values using descriptive names. For example, the dark-photon label equals twelve, and the top-quark label equals one-half.

