## 4. Kernel Classification

This section classifies the pointwise stabilizer of each Klein orbit. The stabilizer of an orbit is the set of group elements that fix every member of the orbit.

### 4.1 Definition

**Definition (Pointwise stabilizer).** For a Klein orbit V-i inside the fourteen-mode multiplet, the pointwise stabilizer is the subgroup of the Klein group consisting of elements that fix every mode in V-i.

### 4.2 Classification

**Theorem (Kernel Classification Theorem).** The pointwise stabilizer of each Klein orbit is one of the three non-trivial subgroups of the Klein group. The classification is:

| Orbit | Members | Pointwise stabilizer |
|---|---|---|
| V-one | neutrino, electron | identity, product of the two generators |
| V-two | Higgs, Z boson | identity, product of the two generators |
| V-three | down quark, bottom quark | identity, product of the two generators |
| V-four | muon, charm quark | identity, product of the two generators |
| V-five | W boson, top quark | identity, diagonal reflection |
| V-six | dark photon, strange quark, up quark, tau | identity only |

**Proof.** Direct computation of the action of each group element on each orbit. We compute representative cases.

**Orbit V-one, consisting of the neutrino and the electron.** The neutrino is at (zero, four) and the electron is at (four, zero). The diagonal reflection sends the neutrino to the electron, so it does not fix every member of the orbit. The half-period translation sends the neutrino to the electron, so it does not fix every member. The product sends the neutrino to the neutrino and the electron to the electron, so it fixes every member. Hence the pointwise stabilizer is the identity together with the product of the two generators.

**Orbit V-five, consisting of the W boson and the top quark.** The W boson is at (two, two) and the top quark is at (six, six). The diagonal reflection sends the W boson to the W boson and the top quark to the top quark, so it fixes every member. The half-period translation sends the W boson to the top quark, so it does not fix every member. The product sends the W boson to the top quark, so it does not fix every member. Hence the pointwise stabilizer is the identity together with the diagonal reflection.

**Orbit V-six, consisting of the dark photon, the strange quark, the up quark, and the tau.** The dark photon is at (five, seven). The diagonal reflection sends the dark photon to the tau, so it does not fix every member. The half-period translation sends the dark photon to the up quark, so it does not fix every member. The product sends the dark photon to the strange quark, so it does not fix every member. No non-identity element fixes every member of V-six. Hence the pointwise stabilizer is the identity only.

The remaining orbits are computed analogously and yield the classification as in the statement.

### 4.3 The three stabilizer classes

**Corollary (Three stabilizer classes).** The Klein orbits partition into three stabilizer classes:

- **Class one, stabilizer generated by the product of the two generators:** four orbits, V-one, V-two, V-three, V-four.
- **Class two, stabilizer generated by the diagonal reflection:** one orbit, V-five.
- **Class three, trivial stabilizer:** one orbit, V-six.

**Proof.** Immediate from the theorem.

**Remark.** The observer orbit V-five is the unique orbit whose pointwise stabilizer is generated by the chiral reflection. This is consistent with the Chirality Selection Theorem of HCSM-26: the observer pair is the unique fixed locus of the chiral reflection on the fourteen-mode multiplet.

