## 5. The Swap-Pair Bridge Theorem

This section proves that the orbit-class swap pair is the unique edge in the distinguished-pair graph that connects two distinct Klein orbits.

### 5.1 The distinguished-pair graph

**Definition (Distinguished-pair graph).** The distinguished-pair graph of the fourteen-mode multiplet has a vertex for each mode and an edge for each two-element subset that is distinguished by at least one structural criterion. The criteria are: (i) being fixed by an involution of the dihedral group; (ii) being a two-cycle of the diagonal reflection; (iii) being a two-cycle of the half-period translation; and (iv) being the orbit-class swap pair, defined in HCSM-26.

The graph has ten edges, listed in HCSM-26. Its vertex set is a subset of the fourteen modes.

### 5.2 The inter-orbit edges

**Theorem (Swap-Pair Bridge Theorem).** The orbit-class swap pair, consisting of the dark photon and the top quark, is the unique edge in the distinguished-pair graph that connects two distinct Klein orbits.

**Proof.** The ten edges of the distinguished-pair graph are, from HCSM-26: the pair of the neutrino and the electron; the pair of the up quark and the strange quark; the pair of the up quark and the dark photon; the pair of the down quark and the bottom quark; the pair of the muon and the charm quark; the pair of the strange quark and the tau; the pair of the dark photon and the tau; the pair of the dark photon and the top quark; the pair of the Z boson and the Higgs; and the pair of the W boson and the top quark.

We examine each edge.

**Within-orbit edges.** The following nine edges have both endpoints in the same Klein orbit:

- The pair of the neutrino and the electron is inside V-one.
- The pair of the Z boson and the Higgs is inside V-two.
- The pair of the down quark and the bottom quark is inside V-three.
- The pair of the muon and the charm quark is inside V-four.
- The pair of the W boson and the top quark is inside V-five.
- The pairs of the up quark and the strange quark, of the up quark and the dark photon, of the strange quark and the tau, and of the dark photon and the tau are all inside V-six.

**The remaining edge.** The edge consisting of the dark photon and the top quark has its two endpoints in distinct Klein orbits: the dark photon is in V-six, and the top quark is in V-five. Hence this edge is the unique inter-orbit edge.

**Corollary (Bridge structure).** The swap pair, consisting of the dark photon and the top quark, is the unique edge of the distinguished-pair graph that bridges the four-cycle orbit V-six and the observer orbit V-five. It is the only connection between the framework-constant orbit and the observer orbit.

**Proof.** Immediate from the theorem.

